{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed56627177/8/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"20270e3b\" lines=\"7-21\">\n<description>\nConnect two sprites using matching connector pixels, then tight-crop.\n\nInput structure:\n- Background color `bg`, sprite color `obj`, connector color `ctrl` (small, 2 components).\n- Two main foreground sprites, plus possibly tiny detached pixels belonging to one sprite.\n\nRule:\n- Identify the **base** sprite (closest to grid top-left).\n- Translate the other sprite so its connector sits one cell above the base's connector.\n- Stamp the plug onto the base; remove connectors (recolor `ctrl` to `obj`).\n- Output is the tight crop around all non-background pixels.\n\nKey insight: The connector pixels define the unique docking point and direction.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"247ef758\" lines=\"35-46\">\n<description>\nMove colored sprites from the left strip into a framed area at marker-defined targets.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg background; rectangular frame on right with colored markers on its edges; sprites in a narrow left strip separated by a constant-color divider column.\n\nRule:\n- For each color, target rows/cols are where both opposite frame edges share that color. Targets are the Cartesian product.\n- Erase each sprite from the left and stamp it centered at every target. Higher sprites cover lower ones.\n\nKey insight: One sprite replicates to a grid of row × column marker intersections.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b2bc3ffd\" lines=\"8-23\">\n<description>\nMove every object above the solid bottom stripe upward by its own pixel count.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background, exactly one full-width non-background row\nthat acts as a fixed stripe, and one or more 4-connected monochrome objects\nabove that stripe.\n\nRule\nClear each object from its original cells, then translate it straight upward\nby the number of pixels in that object, preserving its shape and color. Leave\nthe full-width stripe unchanged.\n\nKey insight\nEach object's area is also its vertical displacement; the full-width row is a\nfixed baseline, not a moving object.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"7e576d6e\" lines=\"159-181\">\n<description>\nDraw the marker-colored route through the centers of all doors in the parallel wall maze.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has a background, parallel full-span walls, one three-pixel door\n  segment in each wall, and two marker pixels sharing a color.\n- Walls are either all vertical or all horizontal, and the markers are on\n  opposite outer sides of the wall set.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the scene unchanged except for the route.\n- Visit the door centers in wall order from the upper/left marker side to\n  the lower/right marker side.\n- In each corridor, draw an orthogonal path to the cell just before the next\n  wall, move along that wall to the door center, cross the center, and\n  continue to the next corridor; after the last door, connect to the other\n  marker.\n\nKey insight:\n- The three-pixel door segments mark the only crossing points through a set\n  of parallel walls, so the output is the corridor path threaded through\n  those centers.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"db695cfb\" lines=\"8-19\">\n<description>\nConnect same-color diagonal pixel pairs, then emit perpendicular rays from differently-colored pixels on those diagonals.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Line color (e.g. blue) has pixel pairs aligned on shared diagonals. Trigger color (e.g. magenta) pixels sit along those diagonals.\n\nRule:\n- Draw diagonal lines connecting each pair of same-color aligned pixels.\n- Where the diagonal crosses a trigger pixel, emit perpendicular diagonal rays (in the trigger's color) extending to the grid boundary.\n\nKey insight: Trigger pixels act as beam-splitters — the perpendicular rays rotate 90° from the connecting diagonal's orientation.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"da6e95e5\" lines=\"70-81\">\n<description>\nExtract the central object from the marker cluster whose markers have the same color.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background and several clusters; each cluster has four\nsame-color corner marker objects around one central monochromatic object.\nRule\nFind every central object enclosed by four aligned corner markers of one color,\nthen choose the unique one whose object color equals the marker color.\nKey insight\nThe answer is not the largest or most frequent object; it is the center of the\nself-colored cluster, cropped to that object's bounding box with background kept.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"68bc2e87\" lines=\"46-60\">\n<description>\nRecover the bottom-to-top stacking order of overlaid rectangular color frames.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is one color, and every foreground color is a one-cell-thick rectangular frame.\n- Overlapping frame pixels show only the color of the frame that is highest at that location.\n\nRule:\n- Reconstruct each frame as the border of the bounding box of its visible pixels.\n- At every cell covered by multiple reconstructed frames, the visible frame color is above the other frames covering that cell.\n- Return the unique bottom-to-top order of frame colors as a single-column grid.\n\nKey insight:\nThe hidden parts of each frame do not need to be seen directly; the visible pixels reveal the full frame bbox, and overlap cells reveal the layer constraints.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7b0280bc\" lines=\"54-69\">\n<description>\nRecolor the shortest path between two terminal squares in a square-and-track graph.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Two non-bg colors form solid NxN squares (N>=2): one color marks exactly 2 terminals, the other marks stations.\n- A third non-bg color forms 1-cell-wide track segments connecting pairs of squares (graph edges).\n\nRule:\n- Find the shortest path (fewest edges) between the two terminal squares.\n- Recolor every station square on that path to green.\n- Recolor every track segment along that path to grey.\n- Everything else unchanged.\n\nKey insight: The grid encodes a graph where squares are nodes and tracks are edges; the task is graph shortest-path, not spatial proximity.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5034a0b5\" lines=\"16-32\">\n<description>\nShift each interior pixel whose color matches a frame side one cell toward that matching side.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a one-cell-thick rectangular frame: the four corners are the\nbackground color, and each side between corners is a solid, distinct side\ncolor. The interior is mostly background with sparse foreground pixels.\n\nRule\nInterior pixels with the top-side color move one row up, bottom-side color\nmove one row down, left-side color move one column left, and right-side color\nmove one column right. A pixel already adjacent to its matching side stays in\nplace; colors that do not match a frame side and the frame itself are unchanged.\n\nKey insight\nThe colored frame sides act as direction labels for matching interior pixels.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"78332cb0\" lines=\"10-23\">\n<description>\nReorder 5×5 blocks into a connected strip by matching edge foreground pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background; sep = divider color (full row/column lines).\n- Several 5×5 sprite blocks in a grid, separated by sep lines.\n\nRule:\n- Two blocks \"connect\" when their facing edges share foreground pixels at the same offset (e.g., A's right edge ∩ B's left edge).\n- Chain all blocks in the orientation (horizontal or vertical) that admits a complete sequence.\n- Output is a 1×N strip of blocks in chain order, separated by sep lines.\n\nKey insight: Edge-contact overlap determines both block ordering and output orientation.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"abc82100\" lines=\"70-86\">\n<description>\nUse local source-target-pattern examples to replace matching source pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- Black background with isolated colored pixels and teal (8) pattern components.\n- A rule example is a 4-neighbor colored pair source->target whose continued\n  direction reaches an anchor touching a teal pattern component.\n\nRule:\n- For each rule example, copy that teal component onto every other pixel of\n  source.color, recoloring the copied component to target.color.\n- Translation maps the rule anchor onto the source pixel being replaced.\n- Remove teal components and all pixels used by rule examples.\n\nKey insight: The two adjacent colored pixels define the color substitution and\nthe anchor point; the nearby teal component is the stencil that gets recolored.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5623160b\" lines=\"44-59\">\n<description>\nPush every colored object touching maroon markers straight outward until it reaches the grid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Maroon pixels are fixed markers.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
